# CORRIGENDUM to two retained logs, both of which stay closed:
#   gen_fu_l37_tag_eor_unconsumed.log            (landing 37, committed bc4eba8)
#   gen_fu_l36_dbg_driver_livelock_supplement.log (landing 37b, committed df1d8c5)
# It answers the Critic's landing-37 FINDING, CM213-Low-2 and CM213-Low-1.
#
# ================ PART 1: the landing-37 log's JUSTIFICATION AND PREDICATE were wrong ================
# The log says "the DUT checks a lookup it consumes" (its lines 11 and 24-27) and its allowance excused a qualified
# tag injection when no record retired at or after the injection's cycle. Neither survives the RTL.
# THE RTL, QUOTED AT FIRST HAND:
#   rtl/ibex_icache.sv:585  assign ecc_err_ic1 = lookup_valid_ic1 & (((|data_err_ic1) & tag_hit_ic1) | (|tag_err_ic1));
#   rtl/ibex_icache.sv:578-582 "Tag ECC across all ways is always expected to be correct so the check does not need to
#     be qualified by hit or tag valid."
#   :470 lookup_valid_ic1 <= lookup_actual_ic0;  :266 lookup_actual_ic0 = lookup_grant_ic0 & icache_enable_i &
#   ~inval_block_cache;  :262 lookup_grant_ic0 = lookup_req_ic0;  :644 ecc_error_o = ecc_err_ic1;
#   rtl/ibex_core.sv:1337 alert_minor_o = icache_ecc_error.
# The tag term is gated by lookup validity ALONE. No term depends on the fetch being consumed or retired, so a tag
# error on any valid lookup alerts one cycle later, a squashed speculative lookup included.
# WHY THE PREDICATE WAS ONLY A PROXY, failing in both directions: it excuses an injection any number of observed
# cycles after the last retirement, so a real missing alert while a stalled core keeps fetching would be counted and
# not failed; and it still fails an injection at the last observed cycle when a record retired in that same cycle,
# which is the false failure landing 37 set out to remove.
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY TRUE OF THE FAILURE: the injection was announced at the run's last clock edge and would have been
# checked on the next one, which never came. The condition is OBSERVABILITY, not consumption.
# THE LESSON, and it is the part worth keeping: landing 37's OUTCOME was right and its REASONING was wrong, which a
# two-sided red cannot catch. Both sides passed because the proxy happened to agree with the truth on the one seed it
# was tested against (the last retirement at 21478 sat just below the last observed cycle). A red proves the rule
# fires and stays silent where it should; it cannot prove the rule is keyed on the right quantity. Only tracing the
# gating terms does that, and I stated a DUT behaviour without tracing them.
# FIXED IN LANDING 39: the allowance is re-keyed on the alert window against the last observed cycle, the in-run
# deferral is removed as unnecessary, and the counter is renamed from unconsumed to unobservable.
#
# ================ PART 2 (CM213-Low-2): the landing-37 log's own provenance and closing claim ================
# Its section 2, side A of the red, pastes its run summaries and names NO root and NO compile digest, while its
# sections 1 and 3 name theirs. And its closing line claims every figure is read "by the command printed above it",
# which holds for section 1 only: sections 2 and 3 paste a script's output with no command beside each figure.
# THE MISSING PROVENANCE, supplied here: side A ran in the root below at the digest below, and side B, the mutation
# and its ablation, in its own root. Both figures are the LOCAL runner's find-digest over dv/auto_dv env, tb, isa and
# gen_tb, not the committer's gate digest, and no committed gate identity applies to either root.
# This is CM212-Low-2 repeated in the next log I wrote, after being told once. Recorded as such.

